 ],
 "title": "The Formal Semantics of Programming Languages: An Introduction",
 "byline": "Glynn Winskel \u00b7 MIT Press (Foundations of Computing), 1993",
 "note": "PDF page = printed page + 21. \u201cFurther reading\u201d sections are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A classic course on giving programming languages precise mathematical meaning. Using a small imperative language (IMP), Winskel presents operational, denotational and axiomatic (Hoare-logic) semantics and proves them consistent, with the induction principles and fixed-point theory needed. He then develops domain theory and applies it to recursion equations, call-by-value and call-by-name, higher-order eager and lazy languages, full abstraction, information systems and recursive types, and closes with nondeterminism and concurrency (guarded commands, CSP-style processes, CCS, the modal \u03bc-calculus and model checking), plus an appendix on undecidability and G\u00f6del incompleteness."
}